\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We reduce the supplied primary candidate U222 from 222 to \textbf{221 working
states}, retaining two tape symbols, one tape, one head, a genuinely blank
initial tape, and left/right moves only. The complete new table has 442 rows.
The reduction changes one retained transition target and deletes the two rows
of native state 200. Its justification is a two-clause local invariant: the
only predecessor of state 112 writes zero and moves left, and the only
predecessor of state 200 then moves right onto that same zero. State 200
therefore never reads one on any run beginning in state zero. Its zero action
equals the zero action of existing state 209.

\begin{theorem}[New machine-equivalence result]\label{thm:main-equivalence}
Let \(\old\) be the exact U222 native table pinned in Section~\ref{sec:inputs}
and \(\newm\) the delivered native U221 table. PA proves
\[
 \Halt(\newm,\mathrm{blank})\ \longleftrightarrow\
 \Halt(\old,\mathrm{blank}).
\]
More strongly, their finite runs from state 0 are related step for step by
the state map of Section~\ref{sec:proof}, with identical tape and head.
\end{theorem}

