\begin{abstract}
The primary U214 machine is reduced to \textbf{213 working states and 426
binary transitions}. Change native $180/1$ to enter 167 instead of 172,
change $172/0$ to enter 67 instead of 112, and delete state 112. The first
change bypasses a behaviorally redundant entry path. It strengthens the
remaining entry invariant enough to justify the second change. Three
universal path identities establish the bypass and one establishes the
state deletion. Both stages are weakly time-improving. The unchanged
source and theory, together with the supplied U214 calibration, give
$\PA\vdash\neg\Halt(\mathsf{U213},\mathrm{blank})\leftrightarrow\Con(\mathsf U)$.
This is a conventional mathematical proof supported by independently
implemented finite checks, not a newly compiled Lean or Rocq certificate
or a global minimality claim.
\end{abstract}

