\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
The supplied 214-state machine is reduced by one working state without
changing its numerical proof-search program. Eight closed binary-tree
rotations preserve the ordered primitive instructions and their complete
control-flow graph. They expose two physical decoder states whose common
branch can be shared, while the preceding tape symbol selects the other
branch. The resulting literal machine has 213 working states, two symbols,
one tape and one head; every transition moves left or right. Initialization
is part of the machine and starts on an all-zero tape.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
Stage & Working states & Rows & Source sites & Distinct subtrees\\
\midrule
Supplied U214 & 214 & 428 & 3,928 & 198\\
Eight-rotation intermediate \(\Rot\) & 214 & 428 & 3,928 & 199\\
Final U213 & 213 & 426 & 3,928 & 199\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

It is nevertheless necessary to prove more than source equivalence.
The binary interpreter uses physical decoder states in several contexts,
and the initialization shares some of those states. A legitimate
reassociation of source syntax need not produce a legitimate machine by
itself. The argument therefore has four independent obligations:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the two sources have exactly the same ordinal control-flow semantics;
\item the final literal table executes every primitive on its entire
admissible encoded frame, not merely on a few sampled registers;
\item the literal all-zero start reaches the correct first source frame;
\item the state fold preserves complete configurations and halting.
\end{enumerate}
The first obligation concerns a finite program graph, the second an
unbounded family of encoded tapes, the third one finite literal run,
and the fourth a local invariant with arbitrary tape exterior.

\begin{proposition}[Halting equivalence of the fold]\label{prop:foldhalt}
For every initial tape with initial state zero and any initial head
position, \(\Rot\) and \(\New\) halt equivalently.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Induct along the old run, preserving (1) and applying
Lemma~\ref{lem:fold} at each step. If \(t\) old steps have been simulated,
the new synchronizing time lies between \(t\) and \(3t\). At every such
time the states agree under \(\rho\), the heads agree and the complete
tapes agree. An old halt therefore produces a new halt.

