\begin{lemma}[Guard at old 172]\label{lem:172}
Every reachable old configuration in state 172 has
\[
                         t(-2)=1,\qquad t(-1)=0.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The only entrance to 172 is $116/0$, which writes zero immediately left of
the new head. The only entrance to 116 is $216/1$, which writes one one cell
farther left. Both moves are rightward and neither step changes the other
cell. This establishes the two asserted bits.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Conditional guard at old 211]\label{lem:211}
Every reachable old configuration in state 211 scanning zero has
$t(-1)=1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If the last step was $163/1$, it wrote one and moved right, so the conclusion
is immediate.

\subsection{Fresh arbitrary-value primitive derivation}
The final 208-state literal table was passed through the retained
whole-tape symbolic-induction engine, whose two inference modules are
pinned byte-for-byte. Its generic primitive configuration contains:
\begin{itemize}
\item nine arbitrary natural primary registers;
\item an arbitrary natural extra header gap;
\item an arbitrary nonempty finite list of spare register blocks;
\item both zero exteriors and the complete program-counter/header region.
\end{itemize}
The register block of value $v$ is $B(v)=0\,1^{v+1}$.
The engine proves unary traversals by induction on arbitrary run lengths
and proves four generic block/list traversals from the literal table.
Its list normalization uses the finite-word identity
$C(A)1=1B(A)$, where $C(v)=10\,1^v$; the identity follows by induction on
the finite list $A$.

